 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Delivery by self-hosted WebSockets.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over WebSocket connections that the project operates. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because each browser repeats reads on every interval and the board lags by up to that interval. A hosted realtime service was rejected because it adds recurring fees and couples the board to one provider. The project accepts the connection capacity, session, and reconnect work that self-hosting requires, because it keeps the board independent of a realtime vendor and gives fast updates. See [decisions/delivery-approach.md](decisions/delivery-approach.md) for the full comparison.
